{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1301724-dark-ages-foundations","id":1301724,"title":"Dark Ages - Foundations","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-04-01T14:34:38-07:00","id":1301724,"title":"Dark Ages - Foundations","content":"I've gone through so much material to find an answer and I can't understand the Foundation of Spontanaeity. So ill-defined. Can anyone help?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13189-knowledgeseeker","name":"Knowledgeseeker","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-01T16:42:59-07:00","id":1301743,"title":"","content":"I may be able to, but I need to know what doesn't make sense about it to you. Is there an inconsistency in the text, or does it not make sense as to what it represents spiritually, or something else?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-02T10:55:21-07:00","id":1301949,"title":"","content":"The book does not explain all that well what the PHILOSOPHY of Spontaneity is that it's pillars are based on. The only thing I understand from what the foundation represents is the word itself. It's as if there was never any extrapolation on what it's supposed to mean for a mage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13189-knowledgeseeker","name":"Knowledgeseeker","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-03T20:14:26-07:00","id":1302425,"title":"","content":"OK. I think I see the issue and I'm going to try to respond. If my answer doesn't seem to hit the mark, it may be because I'm misunderstanding something or missing a logical step but hopefully this will still help even if I do.<br /> <br /> My read on Spontaneity goes like this - this is the Foundation of living in and embracing the moment. Where other Traditions or Fellowships may preach ideas of self-mastery and self-control, where there seems to be a general push to &quot;go beyond&quot; your instincts and emotions and focus on logic, reason, and planning, where there is arguably a general idea that we must &quot;rise above&quot; nature and the natural world in humanity as a whole, the practitioner of the Old Faith asks, &quot;Why?&quot; Why worry about things that haven't happened yet? Why borrow worry from tomorrow? Why hold back what we clearly were meant to feel? Humans are rational beings, yes, but we also are beings of passion, of desire, and most importantly of limited time. They don't let their emotions rule them, but they don't hold them back, either. They live in and for the moment, and though they may have to take time to prepare a ritual or create an item, the magic is not in the preparation. The magic is in the moment you let the spell take shape. The prep work just helps things get there more easily. The whim struck them to cast the spell; it just so happens that it takes a bit of work to let that whim come to life, just as a child takes time to grow and be born.<br /> <br /> Moreover, it is the Foundation of living in tune with the world around you. The Voices live by the song of God and let it guide them, surrendering to what they believe is the will of a distant power; the Batini strive towards unity for all sentient beings, but that unity is not of this world; and even the Spirit Talkers focus more on the world beyond than on this one, and though they may feel the flow of nature to some extent, it is through the spirits and the Talkers' sensitivity to the Umbra and the creatures within, rather than the flow of the seasons that permeates the world. The Old Faith feels the world around them, immerse themselves in it, and attune their minds, bodies, and spirits to it. This is why the core form of their Pillars takes the form of the seasons. The seasons are both literally different aspects of nature and metaphorically different aspects of life and magic themselves. Their ability to attune to the flow of nature and the world around them allows them to tap into the seasons as symbols... if that is how they see the division of the world around them.<br /> <br /> There is one thing to keep in mind - the Pillars don't necessarily follow DIRECTLY from the Foundation, at least in theme. The Valdrman Foundation is Blot - Sacrifice. There is a connection thematically and mythologically (Odin sacrificed to get the runes himself), but the runes aren't as a result the magic of sacrifice - a Valdrman may define his pillars by other forms of sacrifice for power (one pillar could be Odin for his runic sacrifice, one could be Tyr for his sacrifice of his hand to honor his bargain and keep the world safe, and a third could be Frey for his sacrifice of his magic sword that will cost him his life at Ragnarok so that he could win his lover). Now, the book doesn't quite spell that out as well - there's a sidebar that does say as much, but the Grimoire honestly goes against that in some places (especially with the Valdrman, saying that they are the rune mages and the priestesses tend to be Old Faith or Spirit Talkers). But that is something to keep in mind - the pillars and foundation don't need to exactly match up or follow from one another, as much as they have to make sense as part of a cohesive belief system. Thus, it is important to look not at what they pillars SAY they are (Seasons, archangels, etc) but what they represent. And part of that is a size limitation - the book can't spell out all the possible variations, or even more than one or two overall, between the default set of Pillars and every other possible interpretation.<br /> <br /> The Seasons made good sense for the Old Faith because seasons in Europe were a fairly common phenomenon (unlike, say, Texas, where our seasonal Pillars are Summer to the point of Archmastery, an uncomfortable three dots of winter, some weird one dot pillar that sort of is half spring and half fall, and College Football Season), and moreover one that was represented in a lot of the mythology. If you don't like the seasons, choose the gods from the culture your character comes from that represent similar principles (or rearrange the principals between the gods, goddesses, monsters, beings, or worlds that your character draws upon). The follower of the Tuatha may not recognize a pillar of Spring or Summer but instead honor the creativity and fertility of Brigid and the battle fury of the Morrigan; the secret worshiper of the Hellenic gods who venerates the unions of divine principles may interpret the principals of Winter as the domain of Hades and Persephone, and the passion and fire of Summer as Aphrodite and either her lover Hades or her husband Hephaestus. What matters isn't the for the pillars take - it's that they represent both literal aspects of nature and figurative aspects of the human being at different &quot;seasons&quot; or stages of life, and the follower of the Old One live in tune with those rather than fear them; in that connection, they act with Spontaneity, on whim rather than with the planning of kings and scholars.<br /> <br /> One last thing to keep in mind about Spontaneity - there are subtle and not-so-subtle hints at the connection between the Old Faith and the Fae. Spontaneity, through that lens, can be seen as living as the fae did - ruled by passion and the moment and not by being bound to anything but your word and will. Try looking at the Foundation and pillars through a Changeling lens (or if you have it, the lens of Dark Ages: Fae) and see if that helps clarify it at all.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-04T07:49:22-07:00","id":1302549,"title":"","content":"Your explanation was brilliant. It was wonderful. You hit on exactly what my reading was missing. Thank you.<br /> <br /> The concept of Spontaneity as a Foundation I was able to grasp at the fringes but it was empty enough in my reading that I didn't get it. You filled in the blank spaces and now it FEELS like a more wholesome view. Thanks. Good job!<br /> <br /> On another note, I am a big fan of the Foundations as I love the theme they set for Dark Ages. Restrictive when compared to the Spheres, as some say, but altogether still awesome. I am hoping one day they make a true Mythic Age sourcebook, and if not that, then a 20th Anniversary for Dark Ages. It is due for it in a year or so if I'm not mistaken.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13189-knowledgeseeker","name":"Knowledgeseeker","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-04T12:53:12-07:00","id":1302622,"title":"","content":"Oh, good! I was worried I wasn't getting the idea across well there. Glad that it helped fill in the gaps!<br /> <br /> I doubt that they are going to do a full 20th anniversary for it unfortunately... vampire dark ages got it but it was always more popular, and had more books in either edition than the rest of the DA lines combined, iirc. They did give us a bit of it in the Tal'mahe'ra book but I worry that may be the last we see of it for now <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> But like... if they want to prove me wrong I would not mind.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}